   1: ## PS2Recomp: PlayStation 2 Static Recompiler (Experimental)
   2: 
   3: [![Discord](https://img.shields.io/badge/Discord-Join%20Server-5865F2?logo=discord&logoColor=white)](https://discord.gg/JQ8mawxUEf)
   4: 
   5: Also check our [WIKI](https://github.com/ran-j/PS2Recomp/wiki)
   6: 
   7: 
   8: This project statically recompiles PS2 ELF binaries into C++ and provides a runtime to execute the generated code.
   9: 
  10: ### Modules
  11: 
  12: * `ps2xAnalyzer`: scans ELF/functions and writes TOML config (`stubs`, `skip`, instruction patches).
  13: * `ps2xRecomp`: reads TOML + ELF, decodes R5900 instructions, and generates C++ output.
  14: * `ps2xRuntime`: hosts memory, function registration, syscall dispatch, and hardware stubs.
  15: * `ps2xIOP`: R3000A IRX execution, a virtual IOP kernel, and generic HLE fallbacks.
  16: 
  17: ### Features
  18: 
  19: * Translates MIPS R5900 instructions to C++ code
  20: * PS2-specific MMI and VU0 macro support.
  21: * Single-file or multi-file output.
  22: * Configurable stubs, skips, and instruction patches.
  23: * Instruction-driven syscall handling.
  24: 
  25: ### How It Works
  26: PS2Recomp works by:
  27: 
  28: * Parsing a PS2 ELF file to extract functions, symbols, and relocations
  29: * Decoding the MIPS R5900 instructions in each function
  30: * Translating those instructions to equivalent C++ code
  31: * Generating a runtime that can execute the recompiled code
  32: 
  33: The translated code is very literal, with each MIPS instruction mapping to a C++ operation. For example, `addiu $r4, $r4, 0x20` becomes `ctx->r4 = ADD32(ctx->r4, 0X20);`.
  34: 
  35: ### Current Behavior
  36: 
  37: * `stubs` entries generate wrappers that call known runtime syscall/stub handlers by name.
  38: * `stubs` also supports address bindings with `handler@0xADDRESS` for stripped games (for example `sceCdRead@0x00123456`).
  39: * Address bindings also support generic return handlers for triage: `ret0`, `ret1`, `reta0`.
  40: * Recompiler now tries relocation-symbol auto-binding at callsites (`J/JAL`) before raw address dispatch; when relocation symbol is known (for example `sceCdRead`), it can call runtime handlers without manual address mapping.
  41: * Recompiler discovers additional internal static entry targets and emits `entry_...` wrappers for those addresses.
  42: * For unresolved static `J/JAL` sites, generated code falls back to `runtime->lookupFunction(0x...)`.
  43: * `skip` entries are not recompiled and generate explicit `ps2_stubs::TODO_NAMED(...)` wrappers.
  44: * Recompiled `SYSCALL` now calls `runtime->handleSyscall(...)` with the encoded syscall immediate.
  45: * Runtime syscall dispatch tries encoded syscall ID first, then falls back to `$v1`.
  46: 
  47: ### Requirements
  48: 
  49: * CMake 3.20+
  50: * C++20 compiler (currently tested mainly with MSVC)
  51: * SSE4/AVX host support for some vector paths
  52: 
  53: ### Build
  54: 
  55: ```bash
  56: git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/ran-j/PS2Recomp.git
  57: cd PS2Recomp
  58: 
  59: cmake -S . -B out/build
  60: cmake --build out/build --config Debug
  61: ```
  62: 
  63: ### Usage
  64: 
  65: Preferred workflow for retail or stripped games:
  66: 
  67: 1. Open the ELF in Ghidra.
  68: 2. Run `ps2xRecomp/tools/ghidra/ExportPS2Functions.java`.
  69: 3. Use the exported TOML and CSV map.
  70: 4. Recompile with the exported TOML:
  71: 
  72: ```bash
  73: ./ps2_recomp config.toml
  74: ```
  75: 
  76: Fallback workflow for quick local experiments or ELFs with debug symbol :
  77: 
  78: ```bash
  79: ./ps2_analyzer your_game.elf config.toml
  80: ```
  81: 
  82: See the [Ghidra Workflow](ps2xAnalyzer/Readme.md#3-ghidra-integration-for-retail-and-stripped-games-preferred) for ghdira instructions.
  83: 
  84: Then build generated output and link with `ps2xRuntime`.
  85: 
  86: ### Configuration
  87: 
  88: Main fields in `config.toml`:
  89: 
  90: * `general.input`: source ELF path.
  91: * `general.ghidra_output`: recommended function map CSV exported from Ghidra.
  92: * `general.output`: generated C++ output folder.
  93: * `general.single_file_output`: one combined cpp or one file per function.
  94: * `general.low_memory_mode`: reduce peak output-generation memory by avoiding retained disassembly strings and forcing serial output generation. Generated instruction comments are still emitted; disassembly text is produced while writing each output file instead of being kept in memory.
  95: * `general.output_worker_threads`: number of output-generation workers (clamped to nproc * 2). A positive value uses exactly that many workers. `0` uses `nproc - 1` when at least two hardware threads are available, otherwise serial output generation. `1` forces serial output generation.
  96: * `general.patch_syscalls`: apply configured patches to `SYSCALL` instructions (`false` recommended).
  97: * `general.patch_cop0`: apply configured patches to COP0 instructions.
  98: * `general.patch_cache`: apply configured patches to CACHE instructions.
  99: * `general.stubs`: names to force as stubs. Also accepts `handler@0xADDRESS` to bind a stripped function address directly to a runtime syscall/stub handler. Includes generic handlers `ret0`, `ret1`, `reta0`.
 100: * `general.skip`: names to force as skipped wrappers.
 101: * `patches.instructions`: raw instruction replacements by address.
 102: 
 103: Address binding for stripped ELFs:
 104: 
 105: * Use `handler@0xADDRESS` inside `general.stubs` to map a stripped function start directly to a runtime handler.
 106: * Example: `sceCdRead@0x00123456` binds function start `0x00123456` to `ps2_stubs::sceCdRead(...)`.
 107: * Generic temporary handlers are available: `ret0@0xADDR`, `ret1@0xADDR`, `reta0@0xADDR`.
 108: * Before manual binding, prefer recompilation from a Ghidra-exported TOML/CSV first. The extra boundaries and synthetic entry points are usually more important than manual early triage.
 109: * The address must be the function start in that exact ELF build.
 110: * Addresses are not portable across different games/regions/builds.
 111: * The handler name must exist in runtime call lists (`PS2_SYSCALL_LIST` or `PS2_STUB_LIST`).
 112: 
 113: Example:
 114: 
 115: ```toml
 116: # stripped function binding by address:
 117: stubs = ["sceCdRead@0x00123456", "SifLoadModule@0x00127890"]
 118: # temporary return handlers:
 119: stubs = ["ret0@0x001D9410", "ret1@0x001D5BC8", "reta0@0x0024B7C0"]
 120: # mixed example:
 121: stubs = ["printf", "sceCdRead@0x00123456", "SifLoadModule@0x00127890"] 
 122: ```
 123: 
 124: ### Runtime
 125: 
 126: To execute the recompiled code.
 127: 
 128: `ps2xRuntime` currently provides:
 129: 
 130: * Guest memory model and function dispatch table.
 131: * Some syscall dispatcher with common kernel IDs.
 132: * Basic GS/VU/file/system stubs.
 133: * Foundation to expand and port your game.
 134: * `ps2xIOP` execution of original IRX modules with generic HLE fallbacks.
 135: 
 136: See [IOP emulation](ps2xIOP/README.md) for module execution and the service boundary.
 137: 
 138: ### Game Override Hooks
 139: 
 140: Game overrides are runtime-side, build-scoped patch modules.
 141: 
 142: A game override is C++ code that runs during `loadELF` and can replace EE function bindings by address for one specific game build. IOP RPC/DMA behavior is handled by the `ps2xIOP` emulator and its runtime transport. This is separate from recompilation output and separate from global runtime stubs/syscalls.
 143: 
 144: API:
 145: 
 146: * Header: `ps2xRuntime/include/game_overrides.h`
 147: * Register macro: `PS2_REGISTER_GAME_OVERRIDE(name, elfName, entry, crc32, applyFn)`
 148: * Direct bind helper: `ps2_game_overrides::bindAddressHandler(runtime, addr, "handler")`
 149: 
 150: Use Game Override modules when:
 151: 
 152: * You need per-game/per-build routing or patches without polluting global behavior.
 153: * You need to bind many addresses, or install custom replacement logic for a specific title.
 154: 
 155: #### Recommended Iteration Loop
 156: 
 157: 1. Run with minimal config and no aggressive skipping.
 158: 2. Fix hard blockers first (`function not found`, syscall TODO, critical IO stubs).
 159: 3. Use temporary return stubs only to classify call importance.
 160: 4. Promote temporary fixes to real implementations.
 161: 5. Move per-game hacks into game overrides keyed by ELF metadata.
 162: 6. Re-test from cold boot after each batch.
 163: 
 164: ### Describing Your Game Project
 165: 
 166: A game project built with PS2Recomp can describe itself in a `.recomp.json` file at the root of its repository. Lists of recomp and decomp projects, such as [recomp.board](https://recomp.fyi), read that file instead of guessing the game, system and status from the README.
 167: 
 168: > [!NOTE]
 169: > This file is optional: PS2Recomp does not read it and works the same without it. `.recomp.json` and recomp.board are third-party projects; the PS2Recomp developers have no ties to them.
 170: 
 171: Starter file:
 172: 
 173: ```json
 174: {
 175:   "$schema": "https://recomp.fyi/schema/v1.json",
 176:   "game": "<title as it shipped>",
 177:   "system": "PS2",
 178:   "type": "recomp",
 179:   "toolchain": "PS2Recomp",
 180:   "status": "in-progress",
 181:   "original": { "region": "USA", "serial": "SLUS-20312" }
 182: }
 183: ```
 184: 
 185: * `original` is the release a user must own. On retail discs the ELF is named after the serial (`SLUS_203.12` is `SLUS-20312`), and the prefix gives the region: `SLUS`/`SCUS` USA, `SLES`/`SCES` Europe, `SLPS`/`SLPM`/`SCPS` Japan.
 186: * `status` is one of `exploring`, `in-progress`, `playable`, `released`, `complete`, `paused`. Edit it when the project moves on: a stale status is worse than none.
 187: * Never put a game file, or a link to one, in the file.
 188: 
 189: Other fields (Wikidata item, target platforms, maintainers, links, what help is wanted) and the JSON Schema are in the [specification](https://recomp.fyi/spec).
 190: 
 191: ### Limitations
 192:  
 193: * Performance is very bad for VU and GS
 194: * Hardware emulation is partial and many paths are stubbed.
 195: 
